1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,681
Christmas Eve, nineteen fifty-eight.

2
00:00:02,861 --> 00:00:06,209
A blizzard was burying Mansfield,

3
00:00:06,209 --> 00:00:06,716
Ohio.

4
00:00:06,896 --> 00:00:09,371
My father was snowed in at the

5
00:00:09,371 --> 00:00:11,434
plant, the power was out,

6
00:00:11,434 --> 00:00:13,166
and we had one stove,

7
00:00:13,166 --> 00:00:14,487
one pot of soup,

8
00:00:14,487 --> 00:00:16,962
and nine dollars until payday.

9
00:00:17,142 --> 00:00:19,235
And at ten o'clock that night,

10
00:00:19,235 --> 00:00:21,817
somebody pounded on our front door so

11
00:00:21,817 --> 00:00:23,701
hard the whole house shook.

12
00:00:23,881 --> 00:00:25,857
I was fourteen years old,

13
00:00:25,857 --> 00:00:28,306
and I have never been so scared

14
00:00:28,306 --> 00:00:29,176
in my life.

15
00:00:29,525 --> 00:00:31,817
When my mother opened that door,

16
00:00:31,817 --> 00:00:34,467
there were twelve men standing in the

17
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:36,687
snow, in black leather jackets,

18
00:00:36,687 --> 00:00:39,696
with motorcycles half buried in the drifts

19
00:00:39,696 --> 00:00:40,555
behind them.

20
00:00:40,735 --> 00:00:43,091
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

21
00:00:43,091 --> 00:00:44,061
my mother did,

22
00:00:44,061 --> 00:00:46,625
what the whole street said about her,

23
00:00:46,625 --> 00:00:49,050
and what happened the next morning,

24
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:51,753
which nobody on Elm Street ever forgot.

25
00:00:51,933 --> 00:00:53,053
Stay with me,

26
00:00:53,053 --> 00:00:56,929
sweetheart, because the ending still makes me

27
00:00:56,929 --> 00:00:57,273
cry.

28
00:00:57,623 --> 00:01:00,009
That winter was a hard one for

29
00:01:00,009 --> 00:01:00,805
us, honey.

30
00:01:00,985 --> 00:01:03,379
The plant had cut my father's hours

31
00:01:03,379 --> 00:01:04,200
in the fall,

32
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,594
and my mother was taking in ironing

33
00:01:06,594 --> 00:01:07,962
to pay the coal man.

34
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:10,996
There were no presents under our little

35
00:01:10,996 --> 00:01:13,703
tree that year except two oranges and

36
00:01:13,703 --> 00:01:16,118
a pair of mittens she had knitted

37
00:01:16,118 --> 00:01:17,581
from an old sweater.

38
00:01:17,761 --> 00:01:19,799
Danny and I knew not to ask

39
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:20,780
for anything.

40
00:01:21,129 --> 00:01:23,707
The storm started in the afternoon.

41
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:25,983
By supper, the snow was up to

42
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:27,067
the porch rail,

43
00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:29,596
and the wind was screaming down Elm

44
00:01:29,596 --> 00:01:31,619
Street like a freight train.

45
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:34,017
The lights went out at seven.

46
00:01:34,197 --> 00:01:36,434
My mother lit the kerosene lamp,

47
00:01:36,434 --> 00:01:38,672
put the last of the potatoes and

48
00:01:38,672 --> 00:01:40,489
an onion in a pot of soup,

49
00:01:40,489 --> 00:01:42,377
and told us to put on every

50
00:01:42,377 --> 00:01:43,566
sweater we owned.

51
00:01:43,915 --> 00:01:45,056
At ten o'clock,

52
00:01:45,056 --> 00:01:47,491
we were all three sitting by the

53
00:01:47,491 --> 00:01:48,860
stove in blankets,

54
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:50,533
when we heard engines.

55
00:01:50,713 --> 00:01:52,002
Loud ones.

56
00:01:52,182 --> 00:01:53,623
A whole row of them,

57
00:01:53,623 --> 00:01:56,073
coughing and dying in the snow out

58
00:01:56,073 --> 00:01:57,081
on the street.

59
00:01:57,261 --> 00:02:00,733
Then voices, men shouting to each other.

60
00:02:00,913 --> 00:02:02,852
Then boots on our porch.

61
00:02:03,032 --> 00:02:04,471
Then that pounding.

62
00:02:04,651 --> 00:02:06,699
My mother put her finger to her

63
00:02:06,699 --> 00:02:09,143
lips, and she picked up the fireplace

64
00:02:09,143 --> 00:02:09,539
poker.

65
00:02:09,888 --> 00:02:12,677
She opened the door about four inches,

66
00:02:12,677 --> 00:02:13,998
with the chain on.

67
00:02:14,178 --> 00:02:16,064
A huge man stood there,

68
00:02:16,064 --> 00:02:18,358
with a red beard full of ice

69
00:02:18,358 --> 00:02:20,653
and a face burned raw by the

70
00:02:20,653 --> 00:02:21,063
wind.

71
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:22,883
He took off his hat,

72
00:02:22,883 --> 00:02:24,522
which surprised her,

73
00:02:24,522 --> 00:02:25,506
and he said,

74
00:02:25,506 --> 00:02:27,965
ma'am, I'm sorry to scare you.

75
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:29,864
Our bikes froze up.

76
00:02:30,044 --> 00:02:32,083
We've got two boys out here who

77
00:02:32,083 --> 00:02:33,596
can't feel their hands.

78
00:02:33,776 --> 00:02:36,228
Can we just stand in your hallway

79
00:02:36,228 --> 00:02:37,120
for an hour?

80
00:02:37,469 --> 00:02:40,648
Now, sweetheart, you have to understand what

81
00:02:40,648 --> 00:02:43,321
bikers meant in nineteen fifty-eight.

82
00:02:43,501 --> 00:02:46,078
There'd been stories in all the papers.

83
00:02:46,258 --> 00:02:47,268
Gangs.

84
00:02:47,448 --> 00:02:48,366
Fights.

85
00:02:48,546 --> 00:02:49,811
Towns torn up.

86
00:02:49,991 --> 00:02:51,825
Our neighbor, Mrs.

87
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:55,152
Hadley, was watching from her window across

88
00:02:55,152 --> 00:02:57,567
the street, and I saw her curtain

89
00:02:57,567 --> 00:02:59,690
twitch, and I saw her light a

90
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:02,617
candle and pull that curtain shut tight.

91
00:03:02,797 --> 00:03:05,376
Every other house on the street went

92
00:03:05,376 --> 00:03:05,734
dark.

93
00:03:06,084 --> 00:03:08,297
My mother looked at that man for

94
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:09,126
a long time.

95
00:03:09,306 --> 00:03:11,249
Then she looked past him,

96
00:03:11,249 --> 00:03:13,426
at a skinny boy in the snow,

97
00:03:13,426 --> 00:03:16,612
maybe eighteen, shaking so hard his teeth

98
00:03:16,612 --> 00:03:17,700
were clicking.

99
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:19,723
In a minute I'll tell you what

100
00:03:19,723 --> 00:03:21,872
she said to me afterward about that

101
00:03:21,872 --> 00:03:22,118
boy.

102
00:03:22,298 --> 00:03:23,332
But right then,

103
00:03:23,332 --> 00:03:25,538
she took the chain off the door,

104
00:03:25,538 --> 00:03:26,434
and she said,

105
00:03:26,434 --> 00:03:27,813
take your boots off.

106
00:03:27,993 --> 00:03:29,026
All of you.

107
00:03:29,206 --> 00:03:30,715
And wipe your feet.

108
00:03:31,065 --> 00:03:34,027
Twelve grown men came into our little

109
00:03:34,027 --> 00:03:35,468
front room, honey,

110
00:03:35,468 --> 00:03:36,348
one by one,

111
00:03:36,348 --> 00:03:39,390
stomping and dripping and apologizing.

112
00:03:39,570 --> 00:03:41,532
They filled every inch.

113
00:03:41,712 --> 00:03:43,934
I stood in the kitchen door with

114
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:45,184
my heart pounding,

115
00:03:45,184 --> 00:03:46,851
and I watched my mother,

116
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:47,823
five foot two,

117
00:03:47,823 --> 00:03:49,698
hand a towel to each one of

118
00:03:49,698 --> 00:03:51,920
them like they were boys come in

119
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:52,892
from sledding.

120
00:03:53,242 --> 00:03:56,412
The two youngest couldn't feel their fingers.

121
00:03:56,592 --> 00:03:58,762
My mother sat them by the stove

122
00:03:58,762 --> 00:04:00,722
and put their hands in a pan

123
00:04:00,722 --> 00:04:01,702
of warm water,

124
00:04:01,702 --> 00:04:04,712
never hot, because Grandma Pearl had taught

125
00:04:04,712 --> 00:04:07,092
her that hot water on frozen hands

126
00:04:07,092 --> 00:04:08,562
does terrible damage.

127
00:04:08,742 --> 00:04:10,884
The big man with the red beard

128
00:04:10,884 --> 00:04:13,168
watched her, and he didn't say a

129
00:04:13,168 --> 00:04:13,525
word.

130
00:04:13,876 --> 00:04:16,121
Then she went into the kitchen,

131
00:04:16,121 --> 00:04:17,498
and I followed her.

132
00:04:17,678 --> 00:04:20,142
She looked at that one pot of

133
00:04:20,142 --> 00:04:23,116
potato soup, meant to last us three

134
00:04:23,116 --> 00:04:23,541
days.

135
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:25,033
She looked at me.

136
00:04:25,213 --> 00:04:26,262
And she said,

137
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:29,168
Ruthie, get every bowl and every cup

138
00:04:29,168 --> 00:04:30,298
in this house.

139
00:04:30,478 --> 00:04:32,339
And she put in more water,

140
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:34,272
and the last of the onions,

141
00:04:34,272 --> 00:04:36,277
and a whole box of crackers,

142
00:04:36,277 --> 00:04:37,208
and she said,

143
00:04:37,208 --> 00:04:38,210
it'll stretch.

144
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:40,213
It always stretches.

145
00:04:40,562 --> 00:04:42,960
If you're enjoying this story,

146
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:46,077
sweetheart, tap the like button for me.

147
00:04:46,257 --> 00:04:49,682
It helps more people find Grandma's stories.

148
00:04:49,862 --> 00:04:52,614
Twelve men ate that thin potato soup

149
00:04:52,614 --> 00:04:55,365
out of coffee cups and jelly glasses

150
00:04:55,365 --> 00:04:57,200
and Danny's cereal bowl.

151
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,539
Nobody said it was thin.

152
00:04:59,719 --> 00:05:02,413
The red-bearded man washed his cup at

153
00:05:02,413 --> 00:05:04,015
our sink and dried it.

154
00:05:04,364 --> 00:05:06,222
His name was Frank.

155
00:05:06,402 --> 00:05:08,547
He told my mother they were a

156
00:05:08,547 --> 00:05:10,100
club from Pittsburgh,

157
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,393
riding to a funeral in Indiana.

158
00:05:12,573 --> 00:05:14,857
One of their members had been killed

159
00:05:14,857 --> 00:05:16,254
in a factory accident,

160
00:05:16,254 --> 00:05:18,728
and they'd promised his widow they'd be

161
00:05:18,728 --> 00:05:19,109
there.

162
00:05:19,289 --> 00:05:21,402
They knew the storm was coming.

163
00:05:21,582 --> 00:05:22,941
They rode anyway.

164
00:05:23,121 --> 00:05:25,028
My mother asked him why,

165
00:05:25,028 --> 00:05:25,982
and he said,

166
00:05:25,982 --> 00:05:28,286
because we told her we would,

167
00:05:28,286 --> 00:05:28,763
ma'am.

168
00:05:29,113 --> 00:05:31,705
Then Frank noticed our tree,

169
00:05:31,705 --> 00:05:32,260
honey.

170
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,858
The little bare tree with two oranges

171
00:05:34,858 --> 00:05:35,447
under it.

172
00:05:35,627 --> 00:05:37,418
He looked at it for a long

173
00:05:37,418 --> 00:05:39,485
time, and then he looked at my

174
00:05:39,485 --> 00:05:42,240
mother's hands, red and cracked from the

175
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:44,583
ironing, and at the stack of other

176
00:05:44,583 --> 00:05:47,063
people's shirts folded on the table.

177
00:05:47,243 --> 00:05:48,996
He didn't say anything.

178
00:05:49,176 --> 00:05:51,209
But I saw his face change,

179
00:05:51,209 --> 00:05:54,103
and I didn't understand why until the

180
00:05:54,103 --> 00:05:55,120
next morning.

181
00:05:55,469 --> 00:05:58,269
That night, twelve men slept on our

182
00:05:58,269 --> 00:05:59,868
floor, on our couch,

183
00:05:59,868 --> 00:06:01,068
in the hallway,

184
00:06:01,068 --> 00:06:03,387
and one on the kitchen table.

185
00:06:03,567 --> 00:06:06,056
My mother kept the stove going until

186
00:06:06,056 --> 00:06:07,507
three in the morning,

187
00:06:07,507 --> 00:06:09,719
feeding it the last of our coal,

188
00:06:09,719 --> 00:06:11,240
and then the kindling,

189
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:13,451
and then two old chairs from the

190
00:06:13,451 --> 00:06:13,935
cellar.

191
00:06:14,115 --> 00:06:15,278
I heard her,

192
00:06:15,278 --> 00:06:16,344
sweetheart.

193
00:06:16,524 --> 00:06:19,102
I heard her breaking up those chairs

194
00:06:19,102 --> 00:06:21,250
so those boys wouldn't freeze.

195
00:06:21,599 --> 00:06:25,105
Around midnight, I came downstairs for water,

196
00:06:25,105 --> 00:06:27,520
and the skinny boy was awake by

197
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:28,298
the stove.

198
00:06:28,478 --> 00:06:29,999
His name was Eddie.

199
00:06:30,179 --> 00:06:31,360
He was eighteen,

200
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:33,279
and he had run away from a

201
00:06:33,279 --> 00:06:35,862
bad home in West Virginia two years

202
00:06:35,862 --> 00:06:36,379
before.

203
00:06:36,559 --> 00:06:38,183
He told me he hadn't had a

204
00:06:38,183 --> 00:06:40,493
Christmas Eve inside a house since he

205
00:06:40,493 --> 00:06:41,180
was eleven.

206
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,943
Then he asked me,

207
00:06:42,943 --> 00:06:45,737
is your mama always like this?

208
00:06:45,917 --> 00:06:47,509
And I said,

209
00:06:47,509 --> 00:06:48,088
yes.

210
00:06:48,268 --> 00:06:49,232
She is.

211
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:52,213
And here's what my mother told me

212
00:06:52,213 --> 00:06:53,648
later about Eddie,

213
00:06:53,648 --> 00:06:55,642
the thing I promised you.

214
00:06:55,822 --> 00:06:57,233
She said, Ruthie,

215
00:06:57,233 --> 00:06:59,224
when I opened that door,

216
00:06:59,224 --> 00:07:01,464
I didn't see twelve bikers.

217
00:07:01,644 --> 00:07:03,802
I saw one shaking boy who was

218
00:07:03,802 --> 00:07:04,918
somebody's son.

219
00:07:05,098 --> 00:07:06,084
And I thought,

220
00:07:06,084 --> 00:07:08,055
if Danny was ever out in the

221
00:07:08,055 --> 00:07:10,308
snow, I'd pray to God some woman

222
00:07:10,308 --> 00:07:11,716
would open her door.

223
00:07:11,896 --> 00:07:13,614
That's all it was.

224
00:07:13,794 --> 00:07:14,874
One boy.

225
00:07:15,223 --> 00:07:16,326
In the morning,

226
00:07:16,326 --> 00:07:17,724
the storm was over,

227
00:07:17,724 --> 00:07:19,782
and the sun came up on three

228
00:07:19,782 --> 00:07:20,738
feet of snow.

229
00:07:20,918 --> 00:07:22,296
My mother woke up,

230
00:07:22,296 --> 00:07:24,134
and the house was quiet.

231
00:07:24,314 --> 00:07:25,754
The men were gone.

232
00:07:25,934 --> 00:07:28,659
Their blankets were folded in a neat

233
00:07:28,659 --> 00:07:30,022
stack by the door,

234
00:07:30,022 --> 00:07:31,309
every single one.

235
00:07:31,489 --> 00:07:33,149
The floor was swept.

236
00:07:33,329 --> 00:07:35,628
The cups were washed and lined up

237
00:07:35,628 --> 00:07:36,673
on the counter.

238
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:38,786
And there was a note on the

239
00:07:38,786 --> 00:07:41,722
kitchen table, held down with my mother's

240
00:07:41,722 --> 00:07:42,867
own salt shaker.

241
00:07:43,218 --> 00:07:44,518
The note said,

242
00:07:44,518 --> 00:07:45,911
thank you, Mrs.

243
00:07:46,091 --> 00:07:47,171
Dorothy.

244
00:07:47,351 --> 00:07:49,023
You didn't have to,

245
00:07:49,023 --> 00:07:50,079
and you did.

246
00:07:50,259 --> 00:07:51,559
We won't forget it.

247
00:07:51,739 --> 00:07:52,610
Frank.

248
00:07:52,790 --> 00:07:54,021
That was all.

249
00:07:54,201 --> 00:07:56,121
My mother read it twice,

250
00:07:56,121 --> 00:07:58,201
and then she put it in her

251
00:07:58,201 --> 00:08:00,360
apron pocket, and she said,

252
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:00,760
well.

253
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,118
And she said,

254
00:08:02,118 --> 00:08:04,110
that's that, children.

255
00:08:04,290 --> 00:08:05,741
Merry Christmas.

256
00:08:05,921 --> 00:08:09,050
Nobody even mentioned that the coal was

257
00:08:09,050 --> 00:08:09,451
gone.

258
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,169
By nine o'clock,

259
00:08:11,169 --> 00:08:13,051
the whole street knew.

260
00:08:13,231 --> 00:08:14,160
Mrs.

261
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:16,248
Hadley was out on her porch in

262
00:08:16,248 --> 00:08:19,300
her housecoat telling everybody that Dorothy had

263
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:21,207
let a gang into her house with

264
00:08:21,207 --> 00:08:22,479
two children inside.

265
00:08:22,659 --> 00:08:24,488
She said it right where we could

266
00:08:24,488 --> 00:08:24,946
hear it.

267
00:08:25,126 --> 00:08:26,967
She said, mark my words,

268
00:08:26,967 --> 00:08:28,655
they cased that house.

269
00:08:28,835 --> 00:08:29,915
They'll be back.

270
00:08:30,095 --> 00:08:31,654
And she was right,

271
00:08:31,654 --> 00:08:32,173
honey.

272
00:08:32,353 --> 00:08:33,688
They came back.

273
00:08:33,868 --> 00:08:35,668
But not the way she thought.

274
00:08:36,018 --> 00:08:39,073
At two o'clock on Christmas afternoon,

275
00:08:39,073 --> 00:08:41,002
Danny was at the window,

276
00:08:41,002 --> 00:08:42,128
and he yelled,

277
00:08:42,128 --> 00:08:42,449
Mom!

278
00:08:42,629 --> 00:08:44,313
Mom, they're back!

279
00:08:44,493 --> 00:08:45,747
And we heard it.

280
00:08:45,927 --> 00:08:46,972
Engines.

281
00:08:47,152 --> 00:08:48,963
Not twelve this time.

282
00:08:49,143 --> 00:08:51,082
It sounded like a hundred.

283
00:08:51,262 --> 00:08:54,025
My mother went white as the snow.

284
00:08:54,205 --> 00:08:56,608
She told us to get upstairs.

285
00:08:56,788 --> 00:08:58,844
And then she went to the front

286
00:08:58,844 --> 00:09:00,969
door, alone, and she opened it,

287
00:09:00,969 --> 00:09:03,231
and she just stood there with her

288
00:09:03,231 --> 00:09:04,602
hand over her mouth.

289
00:09:04,952 --> 00:09:08,187
Coming down Elm Street were Frank's twelve,

290
00:09:08,187 --> 00:09:10,370
and behind them a coal truck,

291
00:09:10,370 --> 00:09:11,574
and behind that,

292
00:09:11,574 --> 00:09:14,509
a pickup truck from the Pittsburgh club

293
00:09:14,509 --> 00:09:16,842
piled so high with boxes it was

294
00:09:16,842 --> 00:09:17,444
swaying.

295
00:09:17,624 --> 00:09:18,826
And behind that,

296
00:09:18,826 --> 00:09:21,907
more motorcycles, from a club in Columbus

297
00:09:21,907 --> 00:09:24,312
that Frank had called from a gas

298
00:09:24,312 --> 00:09:25,890
station that morning.

299
00:09:26,070 --> 00:09:28,911
Every single house on Elm Street had

300
00:09:28,911 --> 00:09:30,647
its curtains open now,

301
00:09:30,647 --> 00:09:31,120
honey.

302
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:32,879
Every single one.

303
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:35,613
The coal truck backed up to our

304
00:09:35,613 --> 00:09:38,228
cellar window and poured in a full

305
00:09:38,228 --> 00:09:39,151
ton of coal.

306
00:09:39,331 --> 00:09:40,596
A whole winter.

307
00:09:40,776 --> 00:09:43,171
The boxes came off the pickup one

308
00:09:43,171 --> 00:09:43,679
by one.

309
00:09:43,859 --> 00:09:44,822
A ham.

310
00:09:45,002 --> 00:09:45,931
A turkey.

311
00:09:46,111 --> 00:09:48,427
Flour, sugar, coffee,

312
00:09:48,427 --> 00:09:50,082
canned peaches.

313
00:09:50,262 --> 00:09:51,783
A sled for Danny.

314
00:09:51,963 --> 00:09:54,561
A pair of real store-bought ice skates

315
00:09:54,561 --> 00:09:55,039
for me.

316
00:09:55,219 --> 00:09:57,180
And a long flat box for my

317
00:09:57,180 --> 00:09:59,367
mother, wrapped in newspaper,

318
00:09:59,367 --> 00:10:02,458
which she didn't open until everybody was

319
00:10:02,458 --> 00:10:02,835
gone.

320
00:10:03,186 --> 00:10:05,149
Eddie, the skinny boy,

321
00:10:05,149 --> 00:10:07,203
came up the steps last.

322
00:10:07,383 --> 00:10:10,213
He was carrying two new kitchen chairs,

323
00:10:10,213 --> 00:10:11,736
one on each shoulder.

324
00:10:11,916 --> 00:10:14,540
He set them down in our kitchen,

325
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:17,327
right where the old ones had been,

326
00:10:17,327 --> 00:10:18,311
and he said,

327
00:10:18,311 --> 00:10:18,639
Mrs.

328
00:10:18,819 --> 00:10:21,257
Dorothy, I heard you in the night.

329
00:10:21,437 --> 00:10:22,650
Then he hugged her,

330
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:25,204
and that tough little runaway cried like

331
00:10:25,204 --> 00:10:27,184
a baby on my mother's shoulder.

332
00:10:27,364 --> 00:10:29,001
And so did she.

333
00:10:29,351 --> 00:10:30,966
So far, the storm,

334
00:10:30,966 --> 00:10:32,851
the soup, the chairs,

335
00:10:32,851 --> 00:10:34,018
and the coal.

336
00:10:34,198 --> 00:10:36,036
But there's one more thing,

337
00:10:36,036 --> 00:10:38,691
and it's the part people never believe.

338
00:10:38,871 --> 00:10:41,421
While the men were unloading,

339
00:10:41,421 --> 00:10:41,773
Mrs.

340
00:10:41,953 --> 00:10:44,452
Hadley came across the street,

341
00:10:44,452 --> 00:10:46,201
in her good coat now,

342
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:48,033
smiling, and she said,

343
00:10:48,033 --> 00:10:50,032
well, isn't this lovely,

344
00:10:50,032 --> 00:10:53,363
Dorothy, I always said those boys looked

345
00:10:53,363 --> 00:10:53,946
decent.

346
00:10:54,126 --> 00:10:57,089
Frank turned around and looked at her

347
00:10:57,089 --> 00:10:57,970
for a long,

348
00:10:57,970 --> 00:10:58,770
long time.

349
00:10:59,121 --> 00:11:00,407
And Frank said,

350
00:11:00,407 --> 00:11:03,579
ma'am, we knocked on your door first.

351
00:11:03,759 --> 00:11:04,955
Last night.

352
00:11:05,135 --> 00:11:07,069
You looked right at us through the

353
00:11:07,069 --> 00:11:09,117
glass and you turned off your light.

354
00:11:09,297 --> 00:11:10,319
Mrs.

355
00:11:10,499 --> 00:11:13,426
Hadley's face went red as a beet,

356
00:11:13,426 --> 00:11:13,958
honey.

357
00:11:14,138 --> 00:11:16,854
She turned around and walked back across

358
00:11:16,854 --> 00:11:18,686
that street without a word,

359
00:11:18,686 --> 00:11:20,926
and she didn't speak to my mother

360
00:11:20,926 --> 00:11:21,673
for a year.

361
00:11:21,853 --> 00:11:24,440
My mother never said one unkind thing

362
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:25,139
about her.

363
00:11:25,319 --> 00:11:26,666
Not once.

364
00:11:27,016 --> 00:11:29,911
That night, after the house was quiet

365
00:11:29,911 --> 00:11:32,259
and warm for the first time in

366
00:11:32,259 --> 00:11:35,155
weeks, my mother opened the long flat

367
00:11:35,155 --> 00:11:35,468
box.

368
00:11:35,648 --> 00:11:37,912
Inside was a winter coat.

369
00:11:38,092 --> 00:11:39,370
A real wool coat,

370
00:11:39,370 --> 00:11:41,476
deep red, with a fur collar,

371
00:11:41,476 --> 00:11:44,860
from the best department store in Pittsburgh.

372
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:48,170
She'd been wearing my father's old work

373
00:11:48,170 --> 00:11:49,614
jacket all winter,

374
00:11:49,614 --> 00:11:51,379
and Frank had noticed.

375
00:11:51,559 --> 00:11:53,774
In the pocket was an envelope with

376
00:11:53,774 --> 00:11:56,250
two hundred dollars and a second note.

377
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,171
The second note said,

378
00:11:58,171 --> 00:12:00,640
for the lady who opened her door.

379
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:02,352
Merry Christmas.

380
00:12:02,532 --> 00:12:04,079
Two hundred dollars,

381
00:12:04,079 --> 00:12:06,863
sweetheart, in nineteen fifty-eight.

382
00:12:07,043 --> 00:12:09,295
That was more than two months of

383
00:12:09,295 --> 00:12:10,421
my father's pay.

384
00:12:10,601 --> 00:12:13,198
My mother sat at the kitchen table,

385
00:12:13,198 --> 00:12:14,682
in her new red coat,

386
00:12:14,682 --> 00:12:16,018
holding that note,

387
00:12:16,018 --> 00:12:18,763
and she cried until my father finally

388
00:12:18,763 --> 00:12:20,989
made it home from the plant at

389
00:12:20,989 --> 00:12:23,140
midnight and found her there.

390
00:12:23,491 --> 00:12:25,599
And it didn't end there,

391
00:12:25,599 --> 00:12:26,126
honey.

392
00:12:26,306 --> 00:12:29,399
Every Christmas Eve for the next thirty-one

393
00:12:29,399 --> 00:12:31,916
years, a card came from Pittsburgh,

394
00:12:31,916 --> 00:12:34,506
signed by Frank and whoever was left

395
00:12:34,506 --> 00:12:35,513
of the twelve.

396
00:12:35,693 --> 00:12:37,204
And every few years,

397
00:12:37,204 --> 00:12:40,076
the engines would come down Elm Street

398
00:12:40,076 --> 00:12:42,570
again, and Frank would sit at our

399
00:12:42,570 --> 00:12:45,744
kitchen table, a little grayer every time,

400
00:12:45,744 --> 00:12:48,162
and eat my mother's potato soup.

401
00:12:48,342 --> 00:12:49,805
The real kind,

402
00:12:49,805 --> 00:12:50,432
thick.

403
00:12:50,612 --> 00:12:53,053
He always said it wasn't as good

404
00:12:53,053 --> 00:12:54,350
as the thin kind.

405
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:56,221
When my mother passed,

406
00:12:56,221 --> 00:12:57,882
in nineteen eighty-nine,

407
00:12:57,882 --> 00:13:01,340
there were thirty-four motorcycles at her funeral.

408
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:02,752
And at the front,

409
00:13:02,752 --> 00:13:04,927
in a suit that didn't fit him,

410
00:13:04,927 --> 00:13:07,101
was Eddie, the skinny runaway,

411
00:13:07,101 --> 00:13:09,782
who was by then a grandfather himself

412
00:13:09,782 --> 00:13:11,884
and a deacon in his church in

413
00:13:11,884 --> 00:13:12,898
West Virginia.

414
00:13:13,078 --> 00:13:15,038
He told the whole room about the

415
00:13:15,038 --> 00:13:16,875
night she broke up her chairs.

416
00:13:17,225 --> 00:13:18,534
I'm eighty-two now,

417
00:13:18,534 --> 00:13:21,635
sweetheart, and I've thought about that night

418
00:13:21,635 --> 00:13:22,600
my whole life.

419
00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:25,196
My mother had nine dollars and one

420
00:13:25,196 --> 00:13:26,048
pot of soup,

421
00:13:26,048 --> 00:13:28,180
and she gave it away to twelve

422
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:31,093
strangers the whole street was afraid of.

423
00:13:31,273 --> 00:13:34,103
She didn't do it because she expected

424
00:13:34,103 --> 00:13:35,174
anything back.

425
00:13:35,354 --> 00:13:37,604
She did it because one boy was

426
00:13:37,604 --> 00:13:39,104
shaking in the snow.

427
00:13:39,284 --> 00:13:41,894
And everything that came back to her

428
00:13:41,894 --> 00:13:43,417
came because of that.

429
00:13:43,767 --> 00:13:45,578
Tell me in the comments,

430
00:13:45,578 --> 00:13:46,030
honey.

431
00:13:46,210 --> 00:13:48,149
Would you have opened that door?

432
00:13:48,329 --> 00:13:50,419
Be honest with Grandma.

433
00:13:50,599 --> 00:13:52,702
And if you have a story about

434
00:13:52,702 --> 00:13:54,298
a stranger's kindness,

435
00:13:54,298 --> 00:13:55,603
I want to hear it.

436
00:13:55,783 --> 00:13:58,211
There are more of my family's stories

437
00:13:58,211 --> 00:14:00,507
in the playlist right there on your

438
00:14:00,507 --> 00:14:02,542
screen, and I'll see you in the

439
00:14:02,542 --> 00:14:03,132
next one.

440
00:14:03,312 --> 00:14:05,170
Good night, sweetheart.

441
00:14:05,350 --> 00:14:06,847
Merry Christmas.
